\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Lowest-order cubical Whitney forms commute with the coboundary}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{Over #{\mathbb R}, let the dimension of the cubical torus
of \ref{fdip-00OK} be #{n\ge 1}, and let #{N\ge 2}. Keep the
coboundary written there. Realize each edge as an interval of
length #{h>0}. That coboundary remains the difference of face
values. It has no factor #{h^{-1}}.}

\p{For a #{p}-cube #{c_I(x)}, the lowest-order cubical Whitney
form is the tensor product of one-dimensional forms. In each
direction #{i} belonging to #{I}, the factor is
#{h^{-1}\,\extd y_i}, supported on that cube's own edge and
equal to zero on every other edge. In each direction outside
#{I}, the factor is the hat of the base vertex. The Whitney
map #{\Wh} sends a real cochain to the linear combination of
these forms with the cochain's values as coefficients. The
de Rham map #{\Rh} integrates a form over each geometric cube.
The cochain operator in the identity is the coboundary
#{\covwedge} of \ref{fdip-00OK}, and the form operator is the
exterior derivative. Then
##{\Wh\,\covwedge=\extd\,\Wh,\qquad \Rh\Wh=\bbI.}
Arnold, Boffi and Bonizzoni denote these forms by
#{Q_1^{-}\Lambda^p}. They arise as the tensor product of the
one-dimensional complex
\citet{(11)–(12), p. 8}{arnoldboffibonizzoni2014cubic}.
At this order their degrees of freedom are the integrals
over faces of the same dimension
\citet{(13), pp. 9–10}{arnoldboffibonizzoni2014cubic},
and the assembled spaces form a subcomplex
\citet{p. 10}{arnoldboffibonizzoni2014cubic}.
The family name is theirs. The two identities on the periodic
torus are proved here.}

\proof{
\p{In dimension one, write #{\lambda_j} for the hat that equals
#{1} at the vertex #{j} and #{0} at every other vertex, and is
affine on each edge. On the edge from #{j} to #{j+1} the hat
#{\lambda_j} falls from #{1} to #{0}, so its derivative is
#{-h^{-1}\,\extd y}. On the edge from #{j-1} to #{j} it rises
from #{0} to #{1}, so its derivative is #{h^{-1}\,\extd y}.
The Whitney form of a #{1}-cochain #{\beta} is
#{\beta(e)\,h^{-1}\,\extd y} on the edge #{e}, and it equals
zero on every other edge. Evaluation of the hats recovers a
#{0}-cochain. The integral of #{h^{-1}\,\extd y} over an
interval of length #{h} is #{1}, and the same form integrates
to #{0} over every other edge, so integrating recovers a
#{1}-cochain. Thus #{\Rh\Wh=\bbI} on the circle.}

\p{The coboundary of a function #{f} has value
#{f(j+1)-f(j)} on the edge from #{j} to #{j+1}, by
\ref{fdip-00OK}. The exterior derivative of
#{\sum_j f(j)\lambda_j} equals
#{(f(j+1)-f(j))\,h^{-1}\,\extd y} on that edge, which is the
Whitney form of the coboundary. Both sides vanish above
degree #{1}. This is the circle in degrees #{0} and #{1}.}

\p{In dimension #{n}, the form of a #{p}-cube is the tensor
product of these one-dimensional forms: the hat of the base
vertex in each transverse direction, and, in each direction
#{i} of the cube, the form #{h^{-1}\,\extd y_i} supported on
that cube's own edge and equal to zero on every other edge.
The integral of this product over a product cube separates
as a product. Each edge factor integrates to #{1} on its own
edge and to #{0} on every other edge. On the cube each
transverse coordinate is constant, so each transverse hat
contributes its value at that cube's transverse vertex:
#{1} at the base vertex and #{0} at every other vertex. The
product of these values is #{1} on the cube itself and #{0}
on every other cube of the same directions, so
#{\Rh\Wh=\bbI}. A form whose directions are not the
directions of the cube pulls back to #{0} on that cube.}

\p{The exterior derivative hits one transverse hat at a time.
On the side where the transverse index increases, the hat
falls, so the derivative contributes #{-h^{-1}\,\extd y_j}.
Passing that factor through the coordinate forms of the
directions in #{I} that precede #{j} multiplies by
#{(-1)^{\sigma}}, where #{\sigma=\sigma(j,I\cup\{j\})} is the
count in the coboundary of \ref{fdip-00OK}. The two factors
give the coefficient #{(-1)^{\sigma+1}}. The resulting form
is supported on the edges of the enlarged cube based at the
same vertex. The coboundary's value on that cube is the same
#{(-1)^{\sigma+1}}. For a horizontal edge #{\sigma=1},
so the value is #{+1}, which is the count in the next
paragraph. On the other side the hat rises, so the
derivative contributes #{h^{-1}\,\extd y_j}. The same passage
multiplies by #{(-1)^{\sigma}}, and the coboundary value on
the enlarged cube one step back is #{(-1)^{\sigma}}. The
coefficient equals that value. The factor #{h^{-1}} brought
by the derivative, together with the #{h^{-p}} from the
#{p} edge
factors, is the #{h^{-(p+1)}} in the Whitney form of a
#{(p+1)}-cochain. Therefore
#{\extd\,\Wh=\Wh\,\covwedge}.}

\p{On the two-torus the degrees are #{0}, #{1} and #{2}. In
degree #{2} the coboundary is zero and the exterior
derivative of the area form is zero. For a horizontal edge
of cochain value #{1}, the square on the side of increasing
transverse coordinate receives #{+1} and the square on the
other side receives #{-1}. That is the coboundary formula,
and the transverse derivative of the hat produces the same
two integrals. The vertical edge is the same count with the
factors exchanged.}
}
